9
00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:39.950
My name's John Gabrieli.

10
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:42.800
This is Introductory to Psychology, 9.00.

11
00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:44.570
This is a course about you.

12
00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:50.920
The entire course is what do we understand in a scientific way about human nature-- how

13
00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:55.430
people's minds work, how people's brains work that supports their mind.

14
00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:00.170
This entire course is about what's a scientific way to understanding how people feel,

15
00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:02.290
think, and act in the world.

16
00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:08.670
And so we're trying to say that we constantly think you must in your everyday life think

17
00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:09.723
about why do you have your preferences, your desires?

18
00:01:09.773 --> 00:01:11.873
What's easy for you?

19
00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:13.280
What's hard for you?

20
00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:15.110
What's delightful for you?

21
00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:18.990
Why do other people behave the way they do?

22
00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:20.040
How do they think?

23
00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:20.720
How do they feel?

24
00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:24.670
And so there's a lot of realms of this that are tough to get to by science.

25
00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:28.790
But what we're going to focus on this semester is where the scientific approach has shed

26
00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:33.670
light in the way that we used to think about experiments and evidence, about how humans

27
00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:38.078
tick. And as we go through this semester, we'll talk about the brain, we'll talk a fair

28
00:01:38.128 --> 00:01:41.453
bit about chapters from this book, The Man Who

29
00:01:41.453 --> 00:01:44.778
Mistook His Wife for a Hat, from Oliver

30
00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:48.630
Sacks. It was a bestseller even when it wasn't [?

31
00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:49.680
a ScienceWare ?] course.

32
00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:50.040
It's a great book.

33
00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:50.970
You'll enjoy it.

34
00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:53.120
Short, really fun chapters.

35
00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:58.110
We'll talk about how we perceive the world; how we see; especially, a little bit, how

36
00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:03.128
we hear; how we think; how we feel; personality; how we differ from one to the other;

37
00:02:03.178 --> 00:02:04.470
and what we're sort of like; and how we behave in the world; development from childhood

38
00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:09.770
and infancy through adolescence, through young adulthood, where 1 you are mostly, through

39
00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:18.670
getting older, where I am; social interaction, how we behave in groups and think about

40
00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:22.920
other people; and variation in the mental health or psychopathology.

41
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.150
And increasingly, we understand that there's a huge number of people who, at some moment

42
00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:32.603
in their life or another, struggle with some aspect of mental health.

43
00:02:32.653 --> 00:02:37.390
And then we'll focus a lot on, not only the psychological aspects of what we study in

44
00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:41.470
terms of behavior, but also the brain basis of that, and think a little bit about to

45
00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:46.550
what extent the mind is what the brain does, to what extent the mind is what the brain

46
00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:50.470
does. And so for every dimension of being a human being that we'll talk about, we'll

47
00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:54.550
also talk about what we understand currently from the neurological and neuroscientific

48
00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:58.750
literature about how the human brain supports and contributes to different aspects of

49
00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:00.283
being a person.

50
00:03:00.333 --> 00:03:06.633
OK. So everybody who works in a certain field thinks that their field is really, really,

51
00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:08.310
really special, right?

52
00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:11.630
So here's why psychology is really, really, really special.

53
00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:17.390
So it's really, really special, I think, most of all, because every endeavor that we

54
00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:23.390
undertake at a university or in society as a whole-- it's about people, right, except

55
00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:25.830
for when we think about the rest of nature.

56
00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:29.030
But people study biology, chemistry, and physics.

57
00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:35.510
And they think, right, that the sun orbits the earth for some period of time.

58
00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:39.710
And then they think it's the other way around currently, right?

59
00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:42.270
OK, so people come up with these conclusions.

60
00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:46.110
Even though we're trying to understand nature, it's people who make certain investments

61
00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:49.690
in economics or behave in a certain way or vote in a certain way.

62
00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:55.690
It's people who make music and appreciate music, make art and appreciate art, read and

63
00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:57.470
write literature, right?

64
00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:01.790
So in all these dimensions, there's something very fundamental about what it is about

65
00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:07.870
the human mind that gives birth to these areas of inquiry and how those areas, domains

66
00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:10.370
of human experience, are enacted.

67
00:04:10.613 --> 00:04:15.510
So my only goal today is to try to convince you in a number of different ways that

68
00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:21.710
2 we're not simple video camera in our minds between our ears, recording the world in

69
00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:26.590
some objective, simple way, that even the simplest, most obvious things are interpretations

70
00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:30.950
of the world around us at many different levels of thought and feeling and perception.

71
00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:35.590
And then our minds, the way our minds are constructed, determines the world that we experience,

72
00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:37.510
that we see, that we act upon.

73
00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:41.270
And even very simple things that we think are pretty objective and simple, right in front

74
00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:46.920
of our eyes, are determined by inferences and deductions that our mind makes, weighing

75
00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:50.910
sources of evidence in the world and coming to conclusions about what's around us, what

76
00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:51.960
we hear, what we see, and how we think.

77
00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:52.833
So let's start with seeing.

78
00:04:52.833 --> 00:04:53.866
If your vision is reasonable, we say we see something, we believe it, right?

79
00:04:53.916 --> 00:04:57.766
So let's start with something very simple-- these lines.

80
00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:10.230
So one of the tough things about psychology is ever since the Internet came into existence,

81
00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:13.370
people know every cool thing there is to know, right?

82
00:05:13.420 --> 00:05:18.070
OK. I can tell you when I began teaching, people said, oh my gosh, I've never seen such

83
00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:19.710
a thing. It's unbelievable!

84
00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:23.070
And then now, it's like two thirds of the class is like, yeah, I've got that on my

85
00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:24.550
computer at home.

86
00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:26.150
We did that in third grade or whatever.

87
00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:30.430
So all I'm saying is enjoy the ones you haven't seen before, don't ruin it for your neighbors

88
00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:33.857
today, because it's harder and harder to surprise the world in a nice way, right?

89
00:05:33.907 --> 00:05:37.470
OK, but let's look at these lines for a moment here.

90
00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:40.670
And perhaps you'll have the sense, and maybe-- is it glaring up there, sir?

91
00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:43.520
Let's see. OK, is that better?

92
00:05:47.733 --> 00:05:49.483
OK. Maybe not.

93
00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:56.140
So you might have the sense that this line is a different length than this line.

94
00:05:57.100 --> 00:06:00.150
And this might be somewhere intermediate, right?

95
00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:03.130
Now you know, because of psychology, it's all a trick.

96
00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:05.870
But what's simpler than the length of a line?

97
00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:08.150
What's more objective in some sense than the length of a line?

98
00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:12.270
But if we look at the actual lengths, they're all literally identical.

99
00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:14.070
But that center part looks different.

100
00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:16.590
So what does it mean for it to look different?

101
00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:21.590
It means our minds are determining as simple a thing as how long a line is depending

102
00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:23.550
on the other information surrounding 3 it.

103
00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:26.050
It's an interpretation in context.

104
00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:28.490
If we're simply looking, the lines will look the same.

105
00:06:28.540 --> 00:06:30.640
Let's try another one.

106
00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:34.843
It's remarkable that those two lines are identical in length.

107
00:06:34.893 --> 00:06:36.993
[LAUGHTER] OK, all right.

108
00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:41.830
It's OK to test the limits of the credibility of the audience, right?

109
00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:42.880
All right. Yeah.

110
00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:47.550
Of course, if our visual system were ludicrously off, we'd be constantly walking into

111
00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:51.430
walls and falling out windows and things like that, right, if we were misestimating at

112
00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:52.480
that length.

113
00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:55.350
So the idea where we have visual illusions-- and I'll show you some more that I think

114
00:06:55.400 --> 00:07:00.910
you'll be impressed by-- it's not that our visual system is messed up or that psychologists

115
00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:02.530
think it's hilarious to trick us.

116
00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:07.910
It's that lots of things our visual system is a brilliant at, but it's brilliant by having

117
00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:10.950
certain laws or principles that it follows.

118
00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:12.480
And we can show this following those principles by seeing that when we mess with the

119
00:07:12.530 --> 00:07:16.030
typical circumstances, those principles calculate the wrong answer.

120
00:07:19.853 --> 00:07:21.510
So here's another one.

121
00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:26.430
So, to most people, which line looks bigger, the one in the middle or the one on the

122
00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:29.128
side? I know you know it's all a trick, right?

123
00:07:29.178 --> 00:07:33.310
OK. What could be more obvious than that this is longer?

124
00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:36.750
It's just a simple line, but if we draw red lines on top of it then move them

125
00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:39.163
over here, they're dead identical.

126
00:07:39.213 --> 00:07:43.763
The central circle-- does one of them, the middle circle, look larger than the other?

127
00:07:43.813 --> 00:07:48.430
Now you already know, intellectually, that it will turn out those two circles in the

128
00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:49.610
middle will be the same.

129
00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:53.163
But you have to convince yourself that it still looks like they're different.

130
00:07:53.213 --> 00:07:54.213
Here there in red.

131
00:07:54.213 --> 00:07:55.670
Here they are next to each other.

132
00:07:55.720 --> 00:07:56.720
They're identical.

133
00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:00.650
Again, this is evidence that, even for a simple thing like the size of a circle, your

134
00:08:00.700 --> 00:08:02.070
mind is making inferences.

135
00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:06.230
And there are principles and laws that it's following that determine what it is you think

136
00:08:06.280 --> 00:08:07.350
that you see.

137
00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:10.550
Here is two monsters chasing each other.

138
00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:12.870
But in fact, they're identical in size.

139
00:08:12.920 --> 00:08:17.820
The 4 perspective cues make the more distant one look much bigger.

140
00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:19.800
This is from Ted Adelson.

141
00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:22.066
This is a beautiful demonstration of an illusion.

142
00:08:22.116 --> 00:08:23.116
Ted Adelson's in the psychology department.

143
00:08:23.116 --> 00:08:24.354
There's a letter A here.

144
00:08:24.404 --> 00:08:28.165
And believe it or not, there's a letter B there.

145
00:08:28.215 --> 00:08:31.630
Let's see if this looks any better when it goes like this.

146
00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:33.780
It doesn't. All right.

147
00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:38.830
So one of the important things about illusions, demonstrations in this class-- and you

148
00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:40.550
will learn this as we go along-- is occasionally they fail, and we come back and discover

149
00:08:40.600 --> 00:08:43.400
what the lesson of that is.

