00:43:13.840 --> 00:43:16.290
would act under certain circumstances.

711
00:43:16.613 --> 00:43:21.470
And then an experiencer group-- that's a group who actually undergoes an experience,

712
00:43:21.520 --> 00:43:23.250
and I'll tell you what that is now.

713
00:43:23.300 --> 00:43:27.230
So in the experiencer group, pretend you were their research participant.

714
00:43:27.280 --> 00:43:31.510
You walk into a room, and you see in that room a black male and a white male.

715
00:43:31.560 --> 00:43:36.150
Now those two are what psychologists, for some reason, have called confederates.

716
00:43:36.200 --> 00:43:37.350
Those are role players.

717
00:43:37.400 --> 00:43:38.400
They know what they're doing.

718
00:43:38.400 --> 00:43:39.710
They have a plan of what they're going to do.

719
00:43:39.760 --> 00:43:43.510
They're going to put on a little show for you.

720
00:43:43.560 --> 00:43:45.950
But you don't know that.

721
00:43:46.000 --> 00:43:50.310
And the black male stands up and leaves the room to get his cell phone, and he gently

722
00:43:50.360 --> 00:43:51.590
bumps the white male's knee.

723
00:43:51.640 --> 00:43:52.670
This is all set up.

724
00:43:52.720 --> 00:43:54.510
You're just sitting there and you see that little bump.

725
00:43:54.560 --> 00:43:57.360
And now, there's three different groups.

726
00:43:57.800 --> 00:43:59.430
One group, that's it.

727
00:43:59.480 --> 00:44:02.750
Nothing else happens-- A small bump, and the person leaves.

728
00:44:02.800 --> 00:44:07.790
A second group-- as you sit there, the black individual leaves the room and the white

729
00:44:07.840 --> 00:44:12.070
individual says, quote, "Typical, I hate it when black people do that." It's meant to

730
00:44:12.120 --> 00:44:14.290
be obviously provocative and racist.

731
00:44:14.340 --> 00:44:18.630
And then what they consider an extreme slur-- the white person in the room playing this

732
00:44:18.680 --> 00:44:23.580
role uses the derogatory word that's meant to be an extreme slur.

733
00:44:24.600 --> 00:44:28.410
So there's one more thing you need to know.

734
00:44:28.460 --> 00:44:31.230
Now, you're sitting there, and you're either in the control group where there's been

735
00:44:31.280 --> 00:44:36.789
the slight bump, or there's been a moderate slur, or an extreme slur in their words.

736
00:44:36.840 --> 00:44:38.230
The black male returns.

737
00:44:38.280 --> 00:44:40.470
Don't forget, he's in on it, and so is that white male.

738
00:44:40.520 --> 00:44:41.520
But you're not in on it.

739
00:44:41.520 --> 00:44:44.630
You just think there was a bump, and something else may have happened, depending on which

740
00:44:44.680 --> 00:44:45.830
condition you're in.

741
00:44:45.880 --> 00:44:50.050
And the experimenter then gives you a survey about how you feel right now.

742
00:44:50.100 --> 00:44:51.990
Sort of like the happiness, but it's not that.

743
00:44:52.040 --> 00:44:53.830
It's like, how do you feel right now?

744
00:44:53.880 --> 00:44:58.910
And then asks you to pick between those two people a partner for an anagram experiment

745
00:44:58.960 --> 00:45:00.550
that you're about to do.

746
00:45:00.600 --> 00:45:03.296
So they're going to ask you-- this is sort of this question you have.

747
00:45:03.346 --> 00:45:07.630
What's the 21 difference or similarity between what you say you're feeling is and what

748
00:45:07.680 --> 00:45:09.430
you really do?

749
00:45:10.600 --> 00:45:13.630
Both things are important, but do they line up, do they not line up?

750
00:45:13.680 --> 00:45:15.150
So here's the results.

751
00:45:15.200 --> 00:45:16.200
Here's a graph.

752
00:45:16.200 --> 00:45:17.270
And here's how this works.

753
00:45:17.320 --> 00:45:22.590
Negative emotional distress the higher the bar, the more you say, I feel really bad about

754
00:45:22.640 --> 00:45:23.790
what's just happened.

755
00:45:23.840 --> 00:45:25.829
I just heard this comment or no comment.

756
00:45:25.880 --> 00:45:27.870
So let's take a look, the higher the bar.

757
00:45:27.920 --> 00:45:31.309
If you heard no comment, here's how you begin.

758
00:45:31.359 --> 00:45:33.670
So let's start with the forecasters.

759
00:45:33.720 --> 00:45:36.590
All of you are forecasters, because you're pretending you're in the situation but you're

760
00:45:36.640 --> 00:45:39.890
not in it. So here is there was no comments.

761
00:45:39.940 --> 00:45:41.790
That's sort of average or something.

762
00:45:41.840 --> 00:45:46.070
And then you said if you heard a racial slur you would feel terrible.

763
00:45:46.120 --> 00:45:48.220
You would feel terrible.

764
00:45:48.800 --> 00:45:51.998
But look at the other students who are randomly picked.

765
00:45:52.048 --> 00:45:54.130
So we don't think it's a difference among students.

766
00:45:54.180 --> 00:45:55.430
Look at these grey bars.

767
00:45:55.480 --> 00:45:57.230
They're pretty flat.

768
00:45:57.880 --> 00:46:02.080
The person on the spot is somehow not processing this.

769
00:46:02.520 --> 00:46:03.934
And they're filling out, I feel average.

770
00:46:03.984 --> 00:46:08.750
You see the split-- the split between the values that the person thinks they would have,

771
00:46:08.800 --> 00:46:12.070
and the values that are responded to on the spot in the moment.

772
00:46:12.120 --> 00:46:13.968
And what we'll talk about later on in social psychology is there's a tough gap, often,

773
00:46:14.018 --> 00:46:19.618
between the values we espouse and how we act when there's especially unexpected, difficult

774
00:46:26.840 --> 00:46:31.270
things. And very often-- if you've had any experience like this-- afterwards, you go,

775
00:46:31.320 --> 00:46:32.870
oh, what I should have done is this.

776
00:46:32.920 --> 00:46:34.710
Or I wish I would've said that.

777
00:46:34.760 --> 00:46:37.950
But that moment is not happening at that moment, probably because you're kind of weirded

778
00:46:38.000 --> 00:46:39.000
out by the whole thing.

779
00:46:39.000 --> 00:46:39.780
What's going on?

780
00:46:39.780 --> 00:46:41.670
Why would the person say this?

781
00:46:41.720 --> 00:46:42.720
Something doesn't seem right.

782
00:46:42.720 --> 00:46:44.230
I can't sort it out.

783
00:46:44.280 --> 00:46:48.790
And so people tend to shrink in terms of making a strong conclusion of what's going on

784
00:46:48.840 --> 00:46:50.550
if something seems unusually provocative.

785
00:46:50.600 --> 00:46:52.870
And you could say, well, OK, that's their attitudes.

786
00:46:52.920 --> 00:46:53.920
But how about their action?

787
00:46:53.920 --> 00:46:55.550
Who do they pick to be their partner?

788
00:46:55.600 --> 00:47:01.900
And again, the people forecasting said, if I was in this situation, I would never pick

789
00:47:03.400 --> 00:47:09.030
that racist white person to be my partner, because that person stinks-- if I was in that

790
00:47:09.080 --> 00:47:14.309
situation. But if the people are in the situation-- 22 look at the grey bars-- pretty

791
00:47:14.359 --> 00:47:17.684
flat. It's a if on the spot, in the

792
00:47:17.684 --> 00:47:21.009
moment, they can't quite process the values they

793
00:47:21.480 --> 00:47:24.230
feel and the action they're going to take.

794
00:47:24.280 --> 00:47:25.297
And we'll talk about that.

795
00:47:25.347 --> 00:47:29.670
And it's very hard, often, in part, to be brave and stand up to things.

796
00:47:29.720 --> 00:47:31.510
It turns out there's a lot of evidence for this.

797
00:47:31.560 --> 00:47:33.190
It's a human nature thing.

798
00:47:33.240 --> 00:47:37.230
It's very hard to be brave and stand up to things when things are kind of weird, because

799
00:47:37.280 --> 00:47:40.070
almost everybody at first thinks, I don't want to make a fool of myself.

800
00:47:40.120 --> 00:47:41.750
I don't want make trouble.

801
00:47:41.800 --> 00:47:43.750
Maybe I'm not getting the whole picture on this.

802
00:47:43.800 --> 00:47:49.070
And we shrink back from acting in a way that aligns with the values that are clearly

803
00:47:49.120 --> 00:47:54.290
shown here. So this, again, is something about human nature that's very weird.

804
00:47:54.340 --> 00:47:56.390
And it's powerful to come into social psychology.

805
00:47:56.440 --> 00:48:00.070
And that's why it's very hard to stand up to things like oppression and bias.

806
00:48:00.120 --> 00:48:05.830
It's very hard to do, because we tend to not act on our values when we're in complicated

807
00:48:05.880 --> 00:48:07.980
situations on the spot.

808
00:48:08.640 --> 00:48:10.870
And there's a tremendous amount of evidence for that.

809
00:48:10.920 --> 00:48:15.190
So again, how we interpret the situation-- very different in our mind when we imagine

810
00:48:15.240 --> 00:48:17.750
we're there, and when we actually sit there.

811
00:48:17.800 --> 00:48:21.249
And so what these researchers say is this is partly why it's been hard to eradicate some

812
00:48:21.299 --> 00:48:26.899
vestiges of stereotypes and racism, because people have a hard time clamping down on

813
00:48:28.760 --> 00:48:30.860
it in the moment.

814
00:48:33.200 --> 00:48:37.470
So that's a tough topic, but we know we want to deal both with things that are less

815
00:48:37.520 --> 00:48:39.710
controversial but also things that touch people's lives in the real world that we live

816
00:48:39.760 --> 00:48:40.805
in. So we talked about a scientific study of the human nature-- mind and behavior-- how

817
00:48:40.855 --> 00:48:41.890
what we see and hear is determined so much but how our mind interprets the world around

818
00:48:41.940 --> 00:48:46.590
us; how we remember things like word lists or stories, that's hugely influenced by what

819
00:48:46.640 --> 00:48:50.140
we expect to see, like in the picture; how

820
00:48:50.140 --> 00:48:53.640
we think we know things like where Reno is

821
00:48:55.120 --> 00:49:00.720
compared to San Francisco; how we think about things like the probability that somebody

822
00:49:05.680 --> 00:49:10.030
else will have the same birthday, that somebody else will in a group; and the relationship

823
00:49:10.080 --> 00:49:12.350
between how we feel and how we act.

824
00:49:12.400 --> 00:49:16.710
The very feelings we have are often disconnected for actions in the moment.

825
00:49:16.760 --> 00:49:19.870
And sometimes that has a sort of a difficult consequence.

826
00:49:19.920 --> 00:49:22.817
And so we'll explore all these things through the semester, all the different facets

827
00:49:22.867 --> 00:49:26.110
23 that we could possibly get through in one semester of what it is to be human, and

828
00:49:26.160 --> 00:49:30.990
where science has showed us something about human nature, the mind, and the brain.

